/T (. Steps of the Program Step 1: Start the program Execution begins from: public static void main\(String[] args\) Step 2: Check command-line arguments The program checks whether exactly 5 marks are entered. if \(args.length != 5\) If the number of arguments is not 5, it displays the usage message and stops. Step 3: Create variables double total = 0; double[] marks = new double[5]; total stores the sum of all marks. marks[] stores the five subject marks. Step 4: Read the five marks The for loop runs from 0 to 4. for \(int i = 0; i < 5; i++\) Each command-line argument is converted from String to double using: Double.parseDouble\(args[i]\) Step 5: Calculate total Each mark is added to total. total += marks[i]; Step 6: Calculate average average = total / 5; Step 7: Display marks A second for loop displays each subject's mark. Step 8: Display total and average Finally, the program displays: Total marks Average marks Step 9: Stop The program finishes execution.)
/E (. Steps of the Program Step 1: Start the program Execution begins from: public static void main\(String[] args\) Step 2: Check command-line arguments The program checks whether exactly 5 marks are entered. if \(args.length != 5\) If the number of arguments is not 5, it displays the usage message and stops. Step 3: Create variables double total = 0; double[] marks = new double[5]; total stores the sum of all marks. marks[] stores the five subject marks. Step 4: Read the five marks The for loop runs from 0 to 4. for \(int i = 0; i < 5; i++\) Each command-line argument is converted from String to double using: Double.parseDouble\(args[i]\) Step 5: Calculate total Each mark is added to total. total += marks[i]; Step 6: Calculate average average = total / 5; Step 7: Display marks A second for loop displays each subject's mark. Step 8: Display total and average Finally, the program displays: Total marks Average marks Step 9: Stop The program finishes execution.)
